  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 133 AM EDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\n*** MILDER WEATHER ARRIVES TUESDAY INTO WEDNESDAY ***\n\nGlobal model guidance suggests our resident boundary will stall over \nthe Lake Cumberland area tomorrow, perhaps allowing a rogue shower \nor two to graze our easternmost CWA. Elsewhere, conditions will \nlikely (90% chance) remain dry, while northerly flow advects a \ncooler and less humid airmass into the region. Forecast highs range \nfrom the mid 70s over southern Indiana to the low-mid 80s across \nsouthern Kentucky. As the cold core of the upper level low swings \noverhead on Wednesday to bring weak synoptic-scale lift, we may wind \nup with a few diurnally-driven showers. In addition, Bufkit time-\nheights from the NAM depict a nearly saturated PBL and reduced \nvisibilities, with a plan view also showing spotty QPF and 0% prob \nice present both Tuesday and Wednesday nights - suggesting we could \nsee some patchy drizzle, or at least a stratus deck.\n\nOnce this pesky low meanders off to the east on Thursday and ridging \nover the central US expands into our neck of the woods, we should \nsee sunshine return, though the continued cool northerly flow will \nlikely (60-80% chance) maintain highs in the 70s and overnight lows \nin the 50s - near normal for late September. Global models and their \nrespective ensembles remain somewhat split on whether the cut off \nlow over the Mid-atlantic/Northeast expands back into our area or we \nremain under the influence of the aforementioned ridge by the second \nhalf of the weekend, but the latter solution seems to be most \npopular at this time, as the NBM brings a 40-60% chance that high \ntemperatures return to the 80s by Sunday and 70-90% chance by Monday.",
